prompt Continue the loop: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates.
think r1 fixed succession, r2 signed the two proofs. r3's crux (from the Journey 12 exploration): a succession must be carriable — handed to an executor, a kin, a court — and checked with no live estate in hand. Mirrored council_sky_bron / skate_group_bron: a purpose-built bounded flat-Bron reader, never a general parser.
think Record shape format data-dignity-v1: header · estate <id> <keeper> <heir> · one hold <item> <disposition> line per willed holding · directive <sig-hex> · acceptance <sig-hex>. A SignedEstate bundles r1's estate + estate_id + the two sigs; a PortableEstate owns its arrays.
think still_dignity_honest rebuilds the will, recomputes r2's digest, and re-verifies both proofs offline; rebuild replays through r2's signed gates and succeeds. The dignity crux carries: the rested set still extinguishes on rebuild (holder_of == rested), heir_held_count == bequeathed_count asserted.
obs The strongest new test is the tampered-mark refusal: flip a rested mark to bequeathed after the crossing and the keeper's directive falls BadConsent — the will's marks travel *under* the signature (via the digest), not beside it. A flipped directive refuses both still_dignity_honest and rebuild. Unknown field, bad header, missing estate, missing proof, bad disposition, bad hex each refuse.
obs GREEN first pass — no build reds this round (the r2 lessons on shadowing and SHA-256 carried). TAME bans + width clean; the flat-Bron hex/tokenizer idiom reused from the sibling Bron apps. Additive: r1 and r2 re-run GREEN. Demo seeds only, no network (serve = custody gate).
